    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    rdfs:label "Text 3 obtains it"@en, "Text 3 erlangt es"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:NippurBowlInversion ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:NippurText3HouseholdKeptFromTheDestroyer ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 3: 1-11, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 127-128)" .

pmc:NippurText4HouseWalledWithBronze a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    rdfs:label "A house sealed at its four corners and walled about with bronze"@en, "Ein Haus, an den vier Ecken gesiegelt und mit Bronze umwallt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The general charm against all evil spirits for a named man and all the people of his house. It opens by calling the bowls covers to hold in sacred angels and all evil spirits and the tongue of impious amulet-spirits, declares them charmed and sealed in each one of the four corners of his house, and states the ground: because well sealed is his house and well armed, and with a great wall of bronze have I surrounded it. The operator adds of himself: what I desire I grasp, and what I ask I take."@en ;
    skos:note "The bowl whose opening lines settled what the whole corpus was for: the editor says they definitely establish the use of these vessels, and it is here that the inversion is read as holding the spirits in. The seals it borrows are the charm with which Enoch was charmed by his wicked brothers and the seal with which the Seven Stars and the Twelve Signs of the Zodiac were charmed unto the great day of judgment."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 4: 1-7, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 133-134)" .

