pmc:NippurText8LilithsDivorcedFromAMarriedPair a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    rdfs:label "The liliths divorced from a married pair and bound not to return"@en, "Die Lilithen von einem Ehepaar geschieden und am Wiederkehren gebunden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The charm for a named man and his wife, made out in the form of a divorce-writ against the liliths. The liliths are to hear and obey and come forth from the house and the dwelling of the couple; they are not to appear to them in their house nor their dwelling nor their bedchamber, nor in dream by night nor in slumber by day; the writ renders them their divorce and their separation; they are sealed with the signet of El Shaddai, with the signet of the house of Joshua bar Perahia and by the Seven that are before him; and they are not to return from this day and forever. The close reads: and be they preserved for life."@en ;
    skos:note "The fullest divorce bowl in the collection and the one from which the abbreviated text 17 derives. It is also where the writ states its own authority twice, once from heaven and once from across the sea, and where the parentage of the liliths is given so that the instrument has two named parties."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 8: 1-17, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 155-156)" .

