pmc:NippurText13BarrenWifeLovedAndBearing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    rdfs:label "A barren wife loved by her husband and brought to bear"@en, "Eine unfruchtbare Frau vom Mann geliebt und zum Gebären gebracht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The charm a childless and neglected wife has made for herself. The mouths of all races, legions and tongues are closed from her; three named angels pity and love and compassionate and embrace her, enter in before all the sons of Adam, clothe her from their clothing with the garment of the grace of God and sit with her on this side and on that; and on the exterior: quickly be enamoured, be enamoured and come, her named husband, to the marrow of his house and to the marrow of his wife; as she was a virgin travailing and bearing not, so may she be fresh myrtle for crowns."@en ;
    skos:note "One of the two love-charms of the collection, and the editor marks the difference between them: here the barren forsaken wife speaks, not the passionate lover. The same couple is the subject of text 1, and the editor reads the liliths bound there as what had prevented the fruit of the union, affecting the man’s love and virility as well as the woman. The last line is broken where a preparation with leaven is named, which he takes for an aphrodisiac recipe."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 13: 1-13, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 178-179)" .

