pmc:NippurText17WomanDivorcesTheLilithsHerself a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    rdfs:label "A woman divorces the liliths herself and obtains release for her sons"@en, "Eine Frau scheidet die Lilithen selbst und erlangt Lösung für ihre Söhne"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the first person: this day above any day, years and generations of the world, I, a named woman, have divorced, separated, dismissed thee, thou Lilith, Lilith of the Desert, Hag and Ghul; the three of you, the four of you, the five of you; naked are ye sent forth, nor are ye clad, with your hair dishevelled behind your backs. They are to hear and go forth and not trouble her in her house, nor her two named children; they are warded off with the curse which Joshua bar Perahia sent; and the bowl binds and seals with the seal of El Shaddai and with the seal of Joshua bar Perahia the healer, healing and release from heaven for three named sons, and all injurers thwarted and frustrated."@en ;
    skos:note "The client is the operator, which happens in four bowls of this collection and in none of them more completely than here: she dates the writ, pronounces the divorce in the first person and names the master only as the authority behind the ban. Her children appear in two different groups, and the editor cannot resolve them into one family."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 17: 1-12, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 190-191)" .

