pmc:NippurText19BodySealedHeadToToe a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    rdfs:label "A man whose body is infested sealed from the top of his head to his toes"@en, "Ein Mann mit besetztem Leib gesiegelt vom Scheitel bis zu den Zehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The whole person and the bedchamber of a named man are charmed and sealed and countersealed with the seven spells which may not be loosed and with the eight seals which may not be broken. More than half the text is an invocation of named deities and powers, the sixty male gods and the eighty female goddesses and the seventy exalted priests of Bagdana among them. Against all demons, devils and evil satans this charm and bowl is sure and its seals established, from whose charm none ever goes forth and from whose control none sallies forth; the demon and the evil lilith which are in his body are bound by charms in earth and by seals in heaven, and the man is sealed from the top of his head to the toes of his feet."@en ;
    skos:note "The one bowl in which the affliction is located in the client’s body rather than in his house, and the one that denies most explicitly that its work could be undone. The charming and the sealing are repeated six times over, each time in the name of a different pair of powers, and the whole series ends in the same clause of expulsion from his body."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 19: 1-20, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 196-197)" .

