pmc:NippurText21HouseAndThresholdSealedFirstOfFour a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AramaicIncantationTextsFromNippur ;
    rdfs:label "House and threshold sealed from every kind of lilith, the first of a set"@en, "Haus und Schwelle gesiegelt vor jeder Art Lilith, die erste eines Satzes"@de ;
    skos:definition "The first of three surviving bowls of one set for one woman. Sealed and countersealed are the house and threshold of a named woman from all evil plagues, from all evil spirits, from the tormentors, from the liliths and from all injurers, that they approach not to her, to her house and threshold, which is sealed with three signets and countersealed with seven seals from every kind of lilith and from all injurers, from this day and forever."@en ;
    skos:note "The three bowls are kept as three claims and not as one, because what they are is three deposits of a single operation and the corpus makes the count of deposits part of the operation. The editor translates the longest of the three and notes that the writer of the second had more room and made a longer inscription."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:JewishPracticalMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur, text 21: 1-4, ed. and trans. J. A. Montgomery (Publications of the Babylonian Section III, Philadelphia 1913, pp. 203-204)" .

